{
  "dom_id": "step-q342036",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190413/assets/a57b9f45194dafc1edbed9276a2dbfba7cf8b3c5ea66aee69c5b77b6328c8f2e.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=5$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}^{′}(5)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=5.$ Applying the above formula with $x=5$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(5) & ≈\\frac{f(5+1)-f(5)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(6)-f(5)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-5}{1} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
